  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nAnother hot and humid afternoon is again underway today with most of \nnortheast KS already approaching the mid 90s as of 1 PM. Mid-level \nwater vapor imagery this afternoon shows a decaying convective \ncomplex entering central KS as a shortwave rounding the upper level \nridge is noted across eastern Kansas. This wave has helped to \nprovide a bit of upper-level support and shear across far eastern \nKansas this afternoon. For the remainder of the afternoon, MLCAPE \nvalues ahead of the shortwave axis will build into the 3000-4000 \nJ/kg range as deep shear increases towards 25-30 knots. Mesoanalysis \nhas already noted minimal surface inhibition across east-central KS \nand looks to be confirmed by visible satellite imagery showing \ntowering Cu from the Flint Hills towards the I-35 corridor. \nScattered storms are expected to develop within this corridor as the \nshortwave moves into the Mississippi Valley this evening. Given the \nthermodynamic environment and inverted-V soundings, the main hazard \nwith any stronger storms will be wind gusts to 60 mph. By the later \nafternoon and early evening hours, best forcing will become focused \nacross western Missouri so this should shift the best storm chances \neast of the area. Could still see some lingering elevated showers \nand isolated storm across far east-central and northeastern KS, \nbut higher confidence in storm coverage will be east of the area.\n\nThe dangerous heat continues into Labor Day with heat indices again \nexpected to build into the 105-110 degree range. Cannot rule out \nsome weak pop-up showers/storms across far east-central KS by the \nmid to late afternoon, but given the lack of upper-level support and \nlow-level forcing, chances are only around 15 percent. \n\nA change in the synoptic pattern is not expected to come until \nWednesday as a trough digging out of the BC/PNW region moves into \nthe the northern Plains. Most guidance depicts this wave to be \nstrong enough to deepen and push a lee cyclone across the Plains \nWednesday. Given the moisture in place, rain and storms along the \nboundary will be likely Wednesday. Some stronger storms along the \nboundary may be possible by later in the afternoon Wednesday across \neast-central KS as CAPE and shear increase. Gusty winds and some \nsmall hail look to be the main hazards in any isolated strong/severe \nstorm. Given the frontal boundary and appreciable cloud cover \nWednesday, most of the area should find some relief in temperatures, \nespecially across northern Kansas as highs top out in the upper 80s. \nFurther south where the boundary will take a bit longer to reach, \ntemperatures may again approach the mid to upper 90s with heat \nindicies in the low triple digits.\n\nThankfully, the remainder of the work week and weekend looks much \ncooler as a modified Canadian airmass moves into the area. Expect \ntemperatures to top out in the upper 80s and low 90s. A more active \nprecipitation pattern looks to set up by the late week and weekend \nwith several waves moving in from the PNW. While any precipitation \nwill be very welcomed, this pattern should at least keep persistent \nupper-level ridging south and west of the region, and hopefully for \nthe remainder of Summer.",
